|> (1) ...
|> (2) I have not signed any waiver of my rights and the machine does not state t
|> that mine or any other user's accounts are monitored. Hence, does the
|> reading of private email and monitoring of the account constitute a violation
|> of my 4th/5th amendment rights/electronic communication privacy act?
|> (3) I do not pay for the account, I got it for being a grad student in the departm
|> department. Does that make it OK for him to inspect my email? Mine is a 
|> state University and hence the computer should be considered public
|> property....

This sort of thing comes up a lot. You may want to get some legal
advice. There may be a university department where you can get 
help. I have read of at least one court decision in which e-mail
was ruled to be subject to the same legal protection as postal
mail, so that its interception, by a system administator, employer,
or otherwise, could be prosecuted as a felony, if you have proof.
Contacting appropriate university administrators may be sufficient.

Of course, this is a new area, and decisions may vary. In principle,
the fact that you do not own the account or machine should not affect
the privacy of your mail, any more than the fact that you may
not own the mailbox you use for postal mail.

