In article <2mnmhd$s1r@eff.org> kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie) writes:

	I'm glad my university and I have a contract under which I pay my
	tuition and fees and they let me have free expression in many
	university forums. I think they do this because
	  1) they see it as helping their mission
	  2) they want my money
	  3) they don't want the legal liability that would come from assuming
			 editorial control of all university forums

	Happily my situtation is not unique. Many (most?) university customers
	(i.e. students) have similar arrangements.

	I concede that many universities have a legal right to take full
	editorial control of all univeristy-owned forums. I'm glad that most
	contract this right away.

At many universities, a computer account is viewed as a privilege,
not a right. And computing services are also expensive. And even
if it were a right, the thing many people overlook is that with
rights come responsibilities. In the case of universities paying
for internet access and granting access to students, there is a
responsibility to show respect for other users.

While universities are loathe to restrict free-speech and free
expression of ideas they may view users of university resources
for the purposes of lambasting others on the internet as wasteful.
Not only does this use up a scarce resource on many campuses but
it may interfere with other student users access to the computing
services because they tie up disk space and terminals for purposes
that amount to little more than invective. Universities are
communities and there are codes of conduct for community behavior
that are meant to protect the rights and privileges of others.

And, it is arguable whether people who only wish to flame other
users on the internet are really exercising free speech but
merely annoying and perhaps harassing others. For example, 
teaching a user to express his/her ideas without resorting to
name-calling and four-letter words may in the end be of
benefit to that user. In the world at large, the inarticulate
and offensive will find little audience for their views. 

And truthfully, the only time that I am really able to freely
say whatever I want is when I control the means by which I
say it. I cannot expect a newspaper to publish everything that
I might want to say. I cannot expect to be able to stand on
any street-corner and express whatever views I want. I cannot
go into any establishment, public or private, and not respect
the rights of others in those establishments. 

Yet, I think that both George Washington U. (if in fact they
yanked Zeke's account) and Hahneman or Columbia which yanked
Sylvia's account, acted hastily. I think they should have
been advised to temper their conduct in a manner that was
more respectful of others. Notice that this doesn't mean that
they couldn't defend the same positions, it's more a matter of
how they defend those positions. They should have been notified
that there were complaints about their use of computing resources
and they should have been warned that their accounts might be
yanked if they didn't behave more responsibly.

Personally, I had no complaint against Zeke's posts. He was
obviously closed-minded and not really open to an exchange or
honest discussion, but that is his problem not mine. And
there are worse offenders on the net who can't seem to utter
a sentence without hurling an invective or an accusation or
a four-letter word. Yet they persist and we try doggedly to
convince them otherwise and to reason with them, to no avail,
until we realize that it's a wasted exercise and we ignore
them. (Or try to.)

Gary Byma

