In article <Cn3LA9.F7C@csn.org>, Robert A. Wyatt <rawyatt@teal.csn.org> wrote:

>What I did say,
>is that if you use language, etc. that the equipment owner finds offensive,
>etc., the owner should be allowed to say that he/she doesn't like it and
>demand that it stop - or terminate the account if necessary.

I agree that, in many cases, the equipment owner can do this, but don't you 
see any problems with this?  

1) You are at the mercy of the equipment owner. Whatever they find
offensive. Columbia U yanked Sylvia's access because she used the word
"fuck" several times in her posts.  It seems that you too have a fondness
of using that word at times, so if you were on their system, your account 
could be toast too. 

2) As someone who works for an equipment owner, I can tell you that we 
get 100 megs A DAY of new news over the wire, and hundreds of outgoing 
posts by users. How could I, in the slightest stretch of imagination, 
even *try* to control what I find personally offensive on the net?  Grep
the outgoing spool for words like "shit" before passing it up-stream?  
I've seen BBS's that do that, and people just write it as sh*t or something.

3) And what abouts posts that I do not act on? I am implicity giving my
stamp of approval to them, no?

4) The equipment owner can't honestly be resposible for what is posted
on the net in general. Even things that are illegal in the USA are 
perfectly legal in other countries. We can't even stop the talk about 
illegal activities -- ignoring the fact that it is possible to talk about
illegal activities and not break any laws (in the US) as long as you don't
DO them....

Usenet is a means of communications, and all the sites that carry do is
receive it and pass it along down the line.  The site in question is no
more able to control the shit that goes down it than the phone company 
(who owns their equipment too) can do so. Like the phone company, our 
users pay to have access to this means of communication here.

Now if a poster is breaking a law, then I will be willing to assist in
any legal actions against the poster.  If a poster is threatening another 
then I will pass the info on to the Dean and they can choose to throw 
them out, press charges, or whatever.  I'm not the net sheriff.

But since this is a public forum, international and massive in scale, you 
can't really attempt to control what you personally find offensive beyond 
simply ignoring it, not reading it, and using kill files.

But regardless, consider this. I know many sites that simply just throw
complaints regarding usenet away and don't even read them -- especially
the larger sites with 50,000 students for example (I have one explicitly
in my mind).    At least I came onto this group to see what was going on
based on your complaint and was willing to discuss the entire issue. I
also took the time to personally answer your complaint.

   
-- 
