The advisor is a sheister.  The only possible exception to making
this conclusion would be that the subject of the thesis, and its
results, could have political consequences (negative) for the 
university or the department (e.g., race research with data
indicating differences in intelligence, promiscuity, etc. ala
Phillipe Rushton).

If the subject of the thesis is not so politically linked, however,
then the most probable explanation is that the student's advisor
wants to control the spin on how the data are interpreted, and/or
that the advisor wants to claim some or all of the credit for
coming up with (a) the idea for the thesis, (b) the methodology,
(c) the leg work (d) the insightfulness of the conclusions, (e)
etc.....

My advice would be for the student to write to whomsoever she
wishes...especially if no harm can come to the university or
the department for doing so.  She will then have little to
worry about w.r.t. the advisor taking credit for her work...though
she may have to find another advisor once she has finished her
degree...which is a good heuristic in academia anyway.

