You might rethink some of what you said, e.g., "a University has the right to
limit the oinions expressed in e-mail from a computer which they own."  By
"right" do you mean legal authority?  In many countries universities would have
the legal authority to limit opinions expressed, not only in e-mail but in
other forums as well.  In South Africa, at least under the outgoing system,
they may indeed have such authority.  But should they have that "right" or
legal authority?

In the U.S., PUBLIC universities ARE limited by law, especially by the
guarantees in the First and Fourteenth Amendments to the U.S. Constitution. 
Whether speech is delivered on the campus mall, in a campus auditorium, or via
its computing system, a public university cannot deny liberty, e.g. speech,
without due process of law.  That severely limits the public university's legal
authority to cancel expression just because it owns the facilities.

I understand from Carl Kadie's many fine comments that most American private
universities extend similar protections through either explicit or implied
contractual agreements with their students and faculty.

Such limits on universities, whether by law or contract, is quite appropriate. 
A major purpose of such institutions is to promote, not restrict, diversity of
opinion.  When they are true to themselves, they promote the use of their
property for such purposes.  Indeed, the test of the commitment to academic
freedom is the institution's willingness to not interfere with the most
offensive speech.  Expression that is inoffensive does not need protection.

That students or faculty should include a disclaimer when expressing opinions,
even inoffensive ones, I agree with.  Universities could legitimately adopt
rules to such effect, although alleged violators of such an established rule
should also receive due process if their access privileges might be revoked for
failure to use a disclaimer.

If my original suggestion about inundating the sender of the obnoxious message
would lead to crashing systems, obviously that is a serious problem.  Carl
Kadie suggested a modification in an earlier post which I would certainly
accept.


