Well, there's always:

	- The research is funded by an external source, which has
	  'right of first refusal' on the results.

	- The research is university-funded, in which case the 
	  university does have some say in the matter.

	- The grant which funded the research (regardless of source)
	  forbids publication of interim (or tangenital) results.

	- The advisor does not wish to release 'work in progress.'
	  (This has been the case in every research environment I've
	  seen.)

	- The advisor believes that further testing is necessary.
	  (Most universities don't like to see their names attached
	  to research that is later proven bogus - remember cold fusion?)

	- The advisor wishes to release *all* research as a whole.

I don't think we can assume ill will on the advisor's part; we don't
know the possible limitations/restrictions being applied to this par-
ticular project.

