From caf-talk Caf Mar 26 10:31:01 1994
From: neutopia@titan.ucs.umass.edu (Doctress Neutopia)
Newsgroups: alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk,alt.mindcontrol,alt.cyberpunk
Subject: THE GREATEST THREAT
Date: 26 Mar 1994 15:17:52 GMT
Message-ID: <2n1jn0$gi7@nic.umass.edu>

                       THE GREATEST THREAT


     Today I am 38 years young, that is, in linear years.  I knew
there were two major decisions I had to make in my life: who to
marry and what to study.  I was brought up to believe that who to
marry was more important than what to study.  A woman could become
instantly rich and gain high social prestige by "catching" the
right man from the right family background.  But things were
changing when I was a young woman during the Vietnam War.  The
second wave of feminism was at its height.  At the dinner table
every night we would watch Walter Cronkite on the CBS news as the
list of American soldiers who had been killed that day rolled down
the TV screen.  

     My father and sister supported the war.  My mother and I did
not.  As our agreements over the war heated up, I called for my
Mother to divorce my father.  How could she go on being married to
a man who was so stupid that he believed the war was right?  He
would say that we should support the president's decision because
the people elected him.  Then when the images of the national guard
hitting anti-war protesters with their batons flashed for a second
on the screen, he still could not see that America had become a
fascist police state.

     So, I pleaded with Mother to divorce him.  At the dinner table
in front of him we would discuss her options.  She was a
traditional housewife '50s who gave up her career as a sociologist
working for the housing authority inspecting public housing for
poor people in order to raise me and my sister.  She said that if
she divorced him, she wouldn't have enough money, so she would have
to go back to work in a nine-to-five job. Mother said she would not
have enough money to do things her upper-middle marriage allowed
her to do like sending me and my sister to school in Europe.  She
felt her best economic situation was to stick with my father.  She
thought about going back to school to get a Master's degree in
history, but she didn't think she could pass the examination tests
and foreign language requirements.  She believed she would have
more freedom if she remained married to her war-supporting husband.

     Throughout the years I asked her, "What do you see in him? 
You watch Masterpiece theater;  he likes to watch football. You
enjoy reading books; he enjoys playing golf with the boys at 
Greensboro Country Club."  Her answer was that she loved him. Even
though they had very different interests, their basic values were
the same.  I swore to myself that I would not have a marriage like
my mother's.  I longed for a marriage of true minds with someone
who I could work and share ideas with for the same cause.

     The other major discussion, which I had been brainwashed to
think was secondary, was what to study.  Again I began asking this
question during the Second Wave of Feminism.  Feminism had
profoundly changed what was expected of the women of my generation.
We could no longer think that we were going to get married and have
babies and this would give us a meaningful life.  We were now
expected to join the workforce. Only the real nerds wanted babies
and the traditional American dream anymore.  The name of the game
now was joining the system and competing with men for power.  This
is what the 'smarter' women of my class began to do as they
graduated from professional schools.  They were now lawyers,
doctors, architects, college professors, businesswomen.  

     But I knew I could never compete.  Being dyslexic, I had a
hard time reading much less being able to make the grades needed to
get into graduate school.  The women who the college educated men
felt were the marriable material were the competitive ones who were
going to earn big salaries and gain prestige.  I felt there was no
hope for me.  My only options looked like I would have to become a
waitress or a babysitter or a cashier at some department store or
a secretary.  But because of the dyslexia, I couldn't really do any
of these things well because my trouble with the three R's--
reading, writing, and arithmetic and you needed these basic skills
in order to get those kinds of jobs.  I had felt lost as I looked
through the want ads.  Nothing seemed exciting or meaningful to me.

     Maybe my mother was right.  Marriage was the best option for
me.  The only women who could be successful women's libbers were
the ones who could get the powerful jobs, and I wasn't smart enough
to get jobs like that.  So, I had no other option other than to
have a mediocre middle-class marriage and a pink-collar job.  But
the thought of being a yes sir "chick" to the patriarchal boss made
me sick to my stomach.  Even though the educational system had
pegged me in the dumb group who was not college bound, I never
thought I was stupid.  I just didn't fit in anywhere. 

     Trying to escape the problem, I became totally addicted to the
TV until the day when I had a revelation.  I would sit in front of
the TV in my rocking chair and rock back and forth switching from
channel to channel.  During the commercials, the rocking would pick
up, so at least it gave me a little more activity than just
passively sitting there. My grandmother thought I was crazy when
she would come into the den and she saw me in front of the TV
rocking at high speed, tuning out as the commercials aired.  Then,
one day, I was watching a show about life in the future and I
stopped rocking all together.  Something was pulling me away for
the TV.  I realized that even though I didn't have the knowledge to
build a TV set, and I didn't have the mind to work out mathematical 
formulae, I was a creative soul and there was a task for me.  I had
a choice to make:  either I could continue rocking my life away in
front of the TV set, or I could become a creative human being.

     I was as much a failure in Church as I was in school.  All the
smart people didn't have any problem memorizing the catechism which
was required in order to become a confirmed Episcopalian.  When it
come to my turn to recite the catechism, I could never remember the
thing in the right sequence, so again, my peers thought of me as a
stupid girl who couldn't memorize the right words. I hated going to
catechism classes because they were so boring and rigid and there
was no time for dreams.  Billy Nut, my childhood sweetheart, was
the first one to recite it correctly. The girls in the class just
wanted to hang out in the women's room and talk about menstruation
which was new to us at the time. But the minister forced us out of
the bathroom and back into Church. He said that we couldn't take
communion if we weren't confirmed members of the church and the
only way we would be confirmed members of the church was if we
successfully recited the catechism.  If not, we would be denied an
intimate relationship with Christ.  I thought there was no
salvation for me, not in school, not in the workforce, not even at
Church.  If you could not answer the right questions and make good
marks on the examination tests, then you just could not succeed in
life.

     I don't really know where my strength came from.  Maybe it was
from the spirit of Albert Einstein who was born on the same day as
I, March 14th.  He, too, was dyslexic.  Whatever it was, I began to
disregard what the education and church had brainwashed me to
believe about myself, that I was stupid.  I began spending time in
the library. There was one word that I concentrated on, a word that
was talked about a lot in the '60s.  That word was Utopia.  My
quest was to discover what Utopia was.  This opened me up to the
major ideas of the world and the door to eternity. I had found
myself.  My calling was clear.  I had recalled the Ideal City and
no one was ever again going to convince me that I was a stupid
female!  I was a dreamer.  In a society where logic and reason
rules, dreamers are considered crazy.  This explained why I always
loved it when my school teachers would go on a tangent and tell us
about their dreams.  Some of them was afraid to do it and when they
did tell us about their personal philosophy, they always apologized
for getting off the subject and not sticking strictly to the text
book.  To society, dreamers where impractical people who could not
deal with the "real world."  

     Several months ago, I was buying a gallon of water at the
Dairy Mart across the street because the tap water around here is
polluted with all kinds of toxic wastes.  The convenience store
also sells paperback books.  I picked up a copy of _The Way Things
Ought To Be_ by Rush Limbaugh.  Having seen his late night talk
show on TV, I knew that he was a poisonous conservative who had a
lot of media power, probably more than Adolf Hitler.  So I opened
up his book curious with what a thug like him had to say.  I was
terrified that he had a chapter on socialist utopians.  But it
seemed ethical to pay money for his book.  So, I thought about
slipping it underneath my coat and walking out of the store.  As I
was walking out of the store, I thought how horrible it would be if
I got caught stealing his book.  I remembered that in some
countries the punishment for stealing was that you could get your
hand chopped off.  Wouldn't that be horrible to have my hand cut
off for stealing Rush's book?

     When I got home, I began reading what the fascist had to say. 
He was cutting down all the principles that I knew were important
to the future of the human race: ecology, vegetarianism,
egalitarianism, animal and human rights, economic freedom, etc.  He
promoted everything that I thought was wrong in the world:
nationalism, capitalism, Christianity, good-old-boy family values,
the military/industrial complex, and the American way of life. For
Rush, the good woman is a church going, home-schooling stay home
mom who obeys the Ten Commandments and the four M's:  Marriage,
Motherhood, Monogamy, and Mortgage.

     In his chapter entitled, "The Socialist Utopians" he says that
the socialist Utopians are the greatest threat to the American way
of life.  He writes, "Theirs is an anti-American credo, which
abhors American political and governmental institutions and this
nations' capitalistic economy.  Their value system is at war with
the Judeo-Christian tradition upon which this country was founded
and is centered in secular humanism and moral relativism." He says
that many of these left-over '60s radicals want to "replace
religion with secular environmentalism" and that many are into
worshiping the Earth Goddess Gaia whom he calls "enviro-religious
fanatics." He writes, "As their emphasis is on this world, they
cling to the belief that man is morally perfectible and that Utopia
on earth is achievable."  

     Well, I guess I would have to say that Rush is right about
this.  I admit it.  I *am* at war with the American way of life and
I am a prophetess of the Gaia Religion.  I do believe that if we
want to save the world we will have to develop a new image of
evolution that is based on the communal respect for the land.  Yes,
Rush is absolutely right.  Rush has finally found his challenger in
me, Doctress Neutopia. A friend said to me while we were walking on
the university campus the other week, that even though the
conservative organizations have been given millions of dollars to
promote their selfish propaganda on the campuses throughout the
United States, we, the ecofeminists, nature-loving people have
humanity on our side.  Our task is to build Neutopia, a world in
which everyone has what they need in order to build meaningful and
creative lives. We can do it.  I know we can. I can see the new
ecocities we can create when we begin to honor Gaia. The way things
ought to be is not the way Rush Limbaugh sees them.  He is a
product of false consciousness.  Rush writes that it is unrealistic
to think we should feed the world. But we have enough food to feed
the world nutritious vegetarian meals.  And it is our moral
obligation to do so.  This will require global education and the
redistribution the wealth to end the tyranny of global capitalism. 

     On my birthday this year, my dear parents who have supported
my research into Neutopian Thought all these years, sent me a bag
full of nuts.  Not long ago, I visited our Chancellor Scott during
his weekly open office hours at the University of Massachusetts. 
I was pleading with him to set up a series of open forums aimed at
discussing institutional racism and sexism as a way of changing the
conservative climate and apathy on campus.  I said that
announcements of the events should be advertised through campus
publications and that the actual events should be broadcast live
over the university radio and cable TV stations.  I even
volunteered my time and energy to organize the events and be the
mistress of ceremonies. If he really wanted to be a successful
educator by making students think, then he ought to heed my advice. 
If not, he would be your typical highly paid bureaucratic
administrator who follows the party line that education is about
economic development.  It was his job to liberate students minds,
not to enslave them by preparing them to become corporate robots
who work for greed and self-interest.  

     Chancellor Scott said that he had been told by a sociologist
that every thirty years or so that there is a major social
upheaval.  He thought the issue which would cause this to happen
was when people started to address issues of class.  "Well, then,"
I said, "lets begin the forum series with a forum about classism
and what kind of world we can build when the intelligentsia is
working for a vision of social equality." 

     But he didn't go for my ideas. He sort of brushed them aside
and said that his office was planning on holding a forum on this
and that.  Then he asked me how my article on "Education With a
Mission?" which I had sent to him by email had been received in the
newsgroups that I had posted it to.  I smiled and said that some of
the jackasses were saying that I was a nut.  They said that anyone
with a name like Doctress Neutopia has got to be a nut. But I
replied, "The nuts of one epoch are the heras and heroes of the
next."  Walking out of Whitmore Administration building which looks
like a cement fortress, I could imagine Whitmore being blown to
smithereens.  Someday real soon education will be free.












