Dimitri Vulis, CUNY GC Math (dlv@CUNYVMS1.GC.CUNY.EDU) wrote:

>When you use a university's computer, you are paying the tuition.  At some
>schools you also pay some kind of "computer fee".  At public schools you
>(and your parents) are further paying taxes to support the school.

scoile@mason1.gmu.edu (Steve Coile) writes:

>You know, this whole argument, and I've heard it many times, is a bunch of
>shit.  Just because you pay tuition and taxes doesn't mean you own the
>school, or that you can do anything you want with their resources.
[...]

True, but paying tuition and fees generally creates a contract and
even private schools cannot arbitrarily change the terms of a
contract.

Also the contract is not just a department's computer policy, but also
the campus-wide student code. Indeed, since the student code is
usually approved by the university senate, chancellor, or trustees, it
takes precedence over any department-level policies. Most student
codes in the U.S. guarantee free expression and due process.

At public universities in the U.S. students have even more protection
because student codes must be consistent with the Constitution's
requirements for due process and free expression. Also, depending on
how the state university gives out computer access, its students may
constitutionally-protected property interest in their accounts. In
legal terms, this is called an "entitlement". In _Goss v. Lopez_ the
Supreme Court defined an entitlement as:

   "not created by the Constitution. Rather, they are created and
    their dimensions are defined" by an independent source such as
    state statutes or rules entitling the citizen to certain 
    benefits. [footnote reference - "_Id._ at 572-3"].

- Carl

