[...]
>   Free-Nets should not ban so-called adult material. They should not
>   stop minors from access it unless their competent legal advisor
>   says they must.

On the panel on internationalization, someone also suggested mandatory
labeling of material that might be illegal in some other jurisdiction.


Some elaborations:

I don't think there is anything wrong with creating a new newsgroup
from an unmoderated newsgroup as long as 1) the new group has a new
name and 2) the selection policy/editorial policy is consistent with
intellectual freedom. As someone at CFP '94 reminded me, however, by
creating a new newsgroup you are giving yourself editorial control and
you may be liable for any material you include.  [See ref].

Removing items from a *moderated* newsgroup and distributing the
result is a different matter. It is a violation or the original
editor's moral rights and (likely) copyright to remove articles from
his or her work without his or her permission.

I don't feel any moral or legal obligation to label material so that
the Chinese government can stop a Chinese person from reading it.
Indeed, I may have a moral obligation to help a Chinese person read
material his or her government wants to censor.

Government-compelled labeling computer material is unnecessary and
unfair. Unnecessary because interested groups and people can ON THEIR
OWN create lists of material that they like or dislike. Interested
groups can also created trademarked seals approval that only material
that meets their criteria can display. Thus, no government involvement
is necessary.

Unfair because government-compelled labeling invariable requires the
creator of material to pay to have his or her material labeled and
then pay to distribute a pejorative label. Also, unfair because
labeling criteria are invariably subjective and thus can not be well
specified in law.

- Carl

